  id: W1_03
  era: "c. 2300\u20131750 BCE"
  title: The 'Dancing Girl'
  text: The 'Dancing Girl'. Only 10.5 centimetres tall, this young woman stands with one hand on her hip and her left arm covered in bangles. She was found at Mohenjo-daro in 1926 and is one of the finest metal sculptures of the Indus Civilisation, cast about four thousand years ago. Lost-wax casting means the figure was first modelled in wax, covered in clay and heated so that the wax ran out, leaving a mould for molten metal. Bangles like hers are still worn across South Asia today.
